<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>

<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name          "btrfs-integrity-recovery">
<!ENTITY author        "iiLaurens">
<!ENTITY version       "2026.07.20d">
<!ENTITY bundleversion "2026.07.20d">
<!ENTITY launch        "Settings/btrfs-integrity-recovery">
<!ENTITY pluginURL     "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/iiLaurens/unraid-btrfs-integrity-recovery/main/plugin/btrfs-integrity-recovery.plg">
<!ENTITY bundleURL     "https://github.com/iiLaurens/unraid-btrfs-integrity-recovery/releases/latest/download/btrfs-integrity-recovery-x86_64-1.txz">
]>

<PLUGIN name="&name;" author="&author;" version="&version;" launch="&launch;" pluginURL="&pluginURL;">

<CHANGES>
##&name;

###2026.07.20d
- Plugin: replace the custom inline help popovers with the standard unRAID/
  Dynamix help system. Every Settings option now uses a `<div class="help">`
  block toggled by the built-in unRAID **Help** button (no custom CSS/JS).
- Plugin: change Target disk(s) from a multi-select dropdown to per-disk
  checkboxes (more intuitive; same `devices[]` POST handling).
- Plugin: revert run-log naming to `run-<timestamp>.log` (no disk-set suffix);
  each run is one combined log covering all selected disks, so the suffix
  added noise without value.
- Plugin: fix a stray premature `</table>` on the Settings page that had
  orphaned the lower option rows outside the table.
- scrub-rs: add an advisory **parity canary** check at scrub start. Before the
  full scrub, scrub-rs reconstructs the target disk's superblock block from the
  other data disks + primary parity (the same machinery recovery depends on)
  and verifies the btrfs magic. A mismatch warns loudly that the array
  config/slot/parity may be misconfigured, but does not abort the run — the
  scrub still proceeds so the operator sees the actual data state.

###2026.07.20c
- Plugin: schedule rework. Removed the `daily` preset (a scrub can take
  multiple days and would overlap its own previous run). Added `monthly`
  (03:00 on the 1st) and a `custom` option that accepts a validated 5-field
  cron time spec (`CRON` config key), so operators can set any sane interval.
- Plugin: add `Stop Scrub` button + `scrub.sh stop` command to terminate a
  running scrub (kills the runner process group and any scrub-rs children).
- Plugin: fix Settings page device discovery — use `shell_exec()` instead of
  `exec()` so multi-disk arrays are no longer truncated to the last disk.
- Plugin: fix uninstall to remove the exact Slackware package name
  (`btrfs-integrity-recovery-x86_64-1`) instead of a no-op glob, and stop the
  schedule before removing the plugin tree.
- Plugin: add inline help popovers to the Settings page.

###2026.07.20
- HDD read-prefetch overhaul (additive on top of 2026.07.19b's
  reader-thread + parallel-checksum pipeline):
  - Metadata-walk prefetch: `walk_leaves` now issues
    `posix_fadvise(POSIX_FADV_WILLNEED)` for the next 2 child pointers
    queued (chunk-map-aware, all DUP/RAID1 metadata stripes hinted)
    before pop()'ing each node. Single hook back-propagates to ALL
    metadata walks (open-time CHUNK/ROOT/DEV sweeps, the once-only
    CSUM-tree header sweep, every per-dev-extent `LazyCsumProvider::range`
    streaming call, and the EXTENT_TREE reconfirm walk). On HDD the
    bulk-data DEV-tree sweep was already sequential; the seek-dominated
    remaining cost was the scattered metadata reads — each `WILLNEED`
    hint is one nodesize block (<=64 KiB), well inside the kernel's 1-MiB
    readahead cap, so the disk starts fetching the next leaf while we
    parse + cross-check the current one.
  - Reader-thread prefetch + page-cache hygiene: the bulk-read reader
    thread (which bypasses `FsReader::read_physical`) now issues
    `advise_willneed(off + len, min(len, 64 MiB))` before each `read_at`
    (restoring the prefetch behaviour `read_physical` has on the inline
    path) and `advise_dontneed(off, len)` after each read, so a multi-TiB
    one-pass scrub does not progressively evict every other cached page
    behind itself as it walks.
  - Cross-dev-extent lookahead: `scrub_dev_tree`'s outer dev-extent loop
    prefetches the first run of dev-extent N+1 before streaming chunk
    N's csum-tree entries — closes the gap where a long BLAKE2/SHA256
    checksum window crosses a chunk boundary into a new `dext` and the
    previous in-chunk prefetch hint has been consumed.
  - `FsReader` exposes `prefetch_logical(&ChunkMap, logical, len)` and
    `evict_physical_range(phys, len)`, plus `pub(crate)` fns
    `advise_willneed` / `advise_dontneed` so the reader thread's own
    dup'd `File` (which does not go through `FsReader::read_physical`)
    can hint / no-op the kernel directly. All hints are advisory,
    gated `#[cfg(unix)]`.
- Memory invariants unchanged: depth-1 channels + MAX_RUN_SECTORS = 64
  MiB per run, peak RAM still <= 2 x 64 MiB = 128 MiB at default cap,
  independent of disk size. Prefetch hints are page-cache effects only,
  not user-space allocations.
- Verified: matrix TOTAL=30 PASS=27 FAIL=0 SKIPPED(unverified)=3,
  byte-for-byte parity with `btrfs check --readonly --check-data-csum`
  preserved.
- Plugin: schedule rework. Removed the `daily` preset (a scrub can take
  multiple days and would overlap its own previous run). Added `monthly`
  (03:00 on the 1st) and a `custom` option that accepts a validated 5-field
  cron time spec (`CRON` config key), so operators can set any sane interval.

###2026.07.19b
- Throughput overhaul:
  - Fix OOM-kill (rc=137) on multi-TB disks: replace the eager
    BTreeMap<u64, Vec<u8>> of every CSUM-tree entry with a lazy,
    bounded-memory `LazyCsumProvider` that walks the CSUM tree on demand
    per dev-extent. Peak RAM bounded by the largest single dev-extent's
    csum span + one leaf buffer, independent of disk size. A separate
    open-time header-only sweep counts metadata-header errors once per
    bad leaf (preserves undercoverage semantics).
  - Reader thread: decouple the bulk disk read from the CPU checksum
    work via a dedicated thread on a dup'd file handle (own seek
    position). Main thread coalesces runs and sends ReadCmds; reader
    does one read_at per run and ships bytes back on a depth-1 channel.
    While the main thread rayon-checksums run N, the reader is already
    reading run N+1 from disk — fully overlaps CPU work with disk I/O
    (closes the 1-MiB fadvise prefetch ceiling: a 64-MiB SHA256 run
    takes ~4 s of CPU, far longer than the disk takes to read 1 MiB).
  - Parallel checksum via rayon for every algorithm (crc32c / xxhash /
    sha256 / blake2): one code path, no per-algo dispatch. Runs of ≥2
    sectors go through par_iter; smaller runs stay serial.
  - Kernel read-ahead via posix_fadvise(POSIX_FADV_SEQUENTIAL) at
    FsReader::new stays as an advisory hint on the reader-side.
- Read order preserved: DEV_TREE-driven ascending-physical walk
  unchanged by the reader thread (FIFO sync_channel), so disk access
  stays sequential within each contiguous run.
- Verified: matrix TOTAL=30 PASS=27 FAIL=0 SKIPPED(unverified)=3,
  byte-for-byte parity with btrfs check on case 12b, all 3 live
  concurrent-churn scenarios (false-positive clean, true-positive
  1 mismatch matching bytenr, dup-one-copy 1 mismatch matching bytenr).

###2026.07.19a
- Fix plugin install hook: bundle scripts (install.sh / uninstall.sh /
  rc script) were packaged without the execute bit, causing the .plg
  install step to fail with "/bin/bash returned 126".

###2026.07.19
- Initial release: ships the scrub-rs + craft-corrupt binaries and a
  Settings page to run / schedule btrfs integrity scrubs.
</CHANGES>

<!--
Install the bundle. It already contains the prebuilt binaries
(scrub-rs, craft-corrupt) plus all plugin files, so there is NO
binary download at install time (we build & ship it ourselves).
-->
<FILE Name="/boot/config/plugins/btrfs-integrity-recovery/install/btrfs-integrity-recovery-x86_64-1.txz" Run="upgradepkg --install-new">
<URL>&bundleURL;</URL>
</FILE>

<!-- Run install logic (binaries are already in place from the bundle). -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="install">
<INLINE>/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/btrfs-integrity-recovery/scripts/install.sh</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- Re-run the same logic on upgrade: upgradepkg re-extracts the bundle,
     but without this Method="upgrade" hook the post-install steps
     (perms, default config, rc restart) would be skipped on version bumps. -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="upgrade">
<INLINE>/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/btrfs-integrity-recovery/scripts/install.sh</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- Uninstall logic. -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/btrfs-integrity-recovery/scripts/uninstall.sh</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
